  {
   "id": 62,
   "q": 5,
   "section": "Mathematics",
   "text": "A retailer marks up a commodity by 25% above its cost price. He then offers a discount of x% on this marked price. If the final selling price equals the original cost price plus half of the initial profit he would have made had there been no discount, what is the value of x?",
   "options": [
    {
     "label": "A",
     "text": "10",
     "correct": true
    },
    {
     "label": "B",
     "text": "12",
     "correct": false
    },
    {
     "label": "C",
     "text": "15",
     "correct": false
    },
    {
     "label": "D",
     "text": "20",
     "correct": false
    }
   ],
   "solution": "Let Cost Price (CP) = Rs. 100.\nInitial Profit (if no discount) = Marked Price - CP = 125 - 100 = 25.\nHalf of this initial profit = 25 / 2 = 12.5.\nTarget Selling Price = CP + Half Initial Profit = 100 + 12.5 = 112.5.\nMarked Price (MP) = 125.\nDiscount Amount = MP - SP = 125 - 112.5 = 12.5.\nDiscount Percentage (x) = (Discount / MP) * 100 = (12.5 / 125) * 100 = 10%."
  },
